E = {
    4: "“How grotesque!” A mask is spread with odd curls above that of an animal’s features, strangely absurd on someone in a light brown vest and white sleeves so filthy, long unwashed in soils of brown and black.",
    10: "Rings. They are blunt. Ricochet. Sickly with spins that turn into swinging feels, further unto the feeling from new front stops. Again, ringing, ricochet head feelings, from swings that spun and turn the darkening picture. File this under the shadows that move with swinging resemblance; it swings over the view, below a man, his moves bring shapes toward that grow once again in size, you couldn’t notice as much before—THUD!",
    12: "“Why does the boy grow emptier!?” he says with a rev of grit and hate. His mask is revealed to be a grey stone rabbit shape.",
    18: "“AHHHHH!!!” Curdles and gurgles with the spirit’s will, breaking its vocal cord constraints like an awakened viper. Sixteen sat behind the window night, fifteen, fourteen, thirteen; his life is flashing alongside red spills from children’s eyes. Not before twelve, it never could have been without beginning. He is symbolic of hatred to something he cannot know, and some eyes can only pour blood onto their painful sights, heavily burdened with madness clarity. That childhood was meant to be mentally beaten flat out by wind inheritance, without understanding before, though sight rushes with answers unlike language.",
    22: "How would one know themselves to be placed in this position? How would one know how to be? There are moments of pause that weigh in between the urges, and they are empty. What of it? Where from? The hived one pushes past the feelings. His anger overtakes again.",
    28: "Don’t know; the rabbit could not have. How does one do such ritualistic abuse? What drove them to do it? Not even the boy could know. The evil reflected in them brings them out further from themselves and deeper into the world. The world breeds dark corners, where humans, angels, and demons beat each other unknown and unknowingly, not understanding. The moment here finds them both in an instant flash, and then darkness overtakes the spark; violence wins. The hived boy is possessed still.",
    32: "Welcome to this outer darkness from inside the boy, where clay skin opens unto its pneumatic possession, the exhale of the one torn wing first by God. The barking of this boy’s volume also rips his own throat, pushed by an unfathomable boiling that comes from violent eternities, whose lostness denies even the daylight. We do not know life without the light, and the bridge unto the light was the boy. When the eyes opened, light entered the one who denied it. This is the now-pretend human until the light of the sun finds its way.",
    40: "“NO!” The eyes grow further paranoid at the teenager’s cutthroat pleas; they flick more rapidly back against an accelerating knowledge beating. His thoughts are no longer like thoughts; he only sees all things in this moment, no longer just the single sense, no more. The sights found him, and from him. The seeing scars a soul that shares its clay housing from now on. The boy collapses.",
    50: "“Yeah…” he says to himself calmly, with a little soreness in his throat.",
    52: "He turns his head towards the window, where the branches sway with a green overtaken by the golden dawn. The stipes, prickling out with thorns, climb up and over the glass. He sees; it is just hard to remember, but he knows something different now. Did he die? He wonders this in the calm. Where he lay, there is a wetness on the hands beneath the shade, where the sun did not catch him before waking, and there is a loss of warmth in his limbs. He looks down and sees blood; he sees the hives still there. He believes those hives weren't there until last night.",
    54: "“Why is this happening to me? What am I? Something feels true.” He thinks out loud, mumbling through a parched throat, warm from the sun.",
}
